1. Scope and definitions

"Project" means the Eidolon code, documentation, translations, game content,
and assets that their copyright holders make available under this license.
"Upstream" means https://github.com/aeml/eidolon, maintained by Robert Mendola,
or a successor repository designated there by him.
"Modified Version" means your adaptation of the Project, including code changes,
translations, features, and new or replacement game content integrated into it:
character and monster models, weapons, armor, equipment, mounts, textures, rigs,
animations, audio, and other assets. Independent services communicating through
ordinary external APIs are not included solely because of that communication.
"Editable Source" means the preferred form for making changes, including source
code, translation files, build and installation instructions and scripts, and
original editable asset files (such as Blender scenes, rigs, animation projects,
and layered textures), together with exports and any custom generation tools
needed to reproduce the version you distribute or operate.

Separately identified third-party material remains governed by its own license.
This license grants only rights the relevant copyright holder can grant; it does
not replace third-party notices or impose new terms on their original material.

2. Permission

Subject to these conditions, each copyright holder grants you a worldwide,
non-exclusive, royalty-free license to use, copy, translate, modify, distribute,
and publicly operate the Project and Modified Versions, for commercial or
noncommercial purposes. You may host servers in any country, charge for access,
offer VIP subscriptions, sell virtual items, and integrate payment providers.
No separate permission or revenue payment to Upstream is required for activities
that comply with this license. Downstream recipients receive these permissions
directly from the applicable copyright holders under this same license.

3. Attribution and notices

Preserve this license and all applicable copyright, attribution, third-party,
and provenance notices in source and distributed copies. In a reasonably
accessible player-facing credits or about screen, and on any public server
website, display "Based on Eidolon by Robert Mendola, original developer and
maintainer" with a link to Upstream where links are supported. Equivalent
translations are permitted if Robert Mendola and Eidolon remain identifiable.
Credit your own contributors too; do not claim their work was authored by Robert
Mendola. Clearly identify modified versions and independently operated servers,
and describe substantive changes in a changelog or equivalent documentation.
No trademark license or right to imply official status or endorsement is granted.

4. Sharing back when distributing or hosting

If you distribute a Modified Version to anyone outside your organization, or
allow anyone outside your organization to interact with it over a network,
you must, no later than that distribution or network availability:

(a) Make the complete Editable Source of that Modified Version available in a
public repository or download, free of charge and under this same license,
including all integrated changes and new or replacement game assets. Provide
a prominent source link to recipients and network users in an accessible
in-game menu or equivalent interface. Keep it current with each deployed or
distributed version and accessible for as long as that version is offered and
for at least three years after its last distribution or public operation.

(b) Notify Upstream through an issue or pull request containing the source link,
version identifier, and a summary of your changes. Update that submission, or a
linked release history, for subsequent versions. A pull request is welcome but
not required: a complete, accessible source submission satisfies this duty even
if Upstream declines to merge it. If Upstream's submission channels are
unavailable, retain the public source and submit when a channel becomes available.

(c) License your integrated contributions under this same license, granting
Upstream and all other recipients the rights in section 2. Obtain the necessary
rights from contributors before including their work. Do not integrate assets
whose terms prevent this sharing and reuse. Existing separately licensed
third-party components may retain their licenses; supply their notices and
source or acquisition instructions as required by their terms. New third-party
assets used as replacement game content must allow the editable-source delivery
and upstream reuse required here.

You retain ownership of your contributions. No copyright assignment, acceptance
of a contribution, or obligation for Upstream to merge or maintain it is implied.
Private development that is neither distributed nor offered to outside network
users does not trigger this section. Once it is shared or hosted, this section
applies to the entire Modified Version, not only to changes you consider useful.

Do not publish passwords, private keys, payment credentials, player records,
personal data, or transaction records. Supply configuration templates instead.
Independent payment services and their proprietary infrastructure need not be
published; your integrated Project-side payment code must be shared. This
exception does not permit hiding Project modifications in a separate process.

5. Downstream terms and monetization

Distribute the Project and Modified Versions under this license without adding
restrictions on the permissions it grants. You may set prices and service terms
for your hosted service, but those terms must not restrict recipients' rights
to the licensed source and assets. Preserve third-party license obligations.
You are responsible for your service, payment agreements, and applicable laws.

The license does not restrict whether a fork sells cosmetics, convenience,
subscriptions, or gameplay advantages. Upstream's cosmetics-only monetization
and refusal to merge paid-power features are contribution policies, not license
restrictions. All integrated modifications remain subject to section 4,
including modifications Upstream would decline to merge.

6. Termination and reinstatement

Permissions terminate automatically if you violate these conditions. They are
automatically reinstated if you cure the violation within 30 days after first
receiving notice of it from a copyright holder, provided this is your first
notice of a violation from that holder. Otherwise reinstatement requires that
holder's written permission. Termination of your permissions does not terminate
the rights of downstream recipients who comply with this license.

